 public trust, presenter control, and team operations; keeps live decisions visible and reversible; and treats setup/recovery as first-class product surfaces rather than help documentation. I’m sending the full sourced handoff and research expansion queue now.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_002a496e23ec3bf4016a7e1963eac08191ae76ae01fd70bace\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_DHTuSBJO4WIRTHtH2OWgBht5|fc_002a496e23ec3bf4016a7e199722ac8191b89e879bed186021","name":"todo","arguments":{"op":"done","task":"Send sourced findings and expansion leads"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_mt4jBaUUiYzDdhhsjKZIGSkv|fc_002a496e23ec3bf4016a7e199722c4819191358ce2ab961cac","name":"task_send","arguments":{"to":"lead","summary":"Complete sourced PWA presentation UX research brief","message":"# PWA presentation UX research brief\n\n## 1. Product model\nUse a lifecycle IA, not a flat dashboard of features:\n\n- **Prepare**: session brief -> content/evidence -> team roles -> displays/devices -> offline pack -> rehearsal/readiness.\n- **Live**: one shared session state projected into three distinct roles: **Public Stage**, **Presenter Console**, and **Team Signals**.\n- **Review**: session summary -> exact public timeline -> evidence ledger -> decisions/incidents -> exports/share.\n\nThe core rule is **one state, role-appropriate disclosure**. The audience screen never exposes notes, messages, connection diagnostics, confidence debate, or unpublished evidence. The presenter console should not mimic the public composition; it is an operational cockpit.\n\n## 2. Surface architecture\n\n### Public Stage\nRequired states: Lobby/starting soon; content; evidence reveal; pause/intermission; reconnecting/holding; session ended/report link.\n\n- Remove app navigation and private chrome. Maintain a stable stage-safe frame.\n- Show only the current approved claim/media, optional captions, source attribution, and deliberately enabled session code/QR.\n- Do not auto-advance evidence or rotate content. A presenter action triggers transitions; respect reduced motion.\n- If data fails, freeze the last valid frame rather than blanking, spinning indefinitely, exposing errors, or jumping backward.\n- “Reconnecting” should be calm, audience-safe copy; technical diagnosis stays in the console.\n- Never use public toasts/chat bubbles over core content. Reserve a bounded lower-third region for evidence/source attribution.\n\n### Evidence cards\nTwo projections from one evidence object:\n\n**Public card**: claim/title; source/publisher; publication date; evidence state label; optional short quotation; accessible source link/QR when appropriate.\n\n**Operator card**: all public fields plus full URL/provenance, captured-at timestamp, excerpt/context, contributor, reviewer, confidence/limitations, duplicate/conflict flags, publish/hold action, and where it has appeared.\n\nRecommended lifecycle: **Draft -> Needs review -> Approved -> Queued -> On screen -> Retired**, always text+icon+shape, never color alone. Put provenance before engagement metrics. Do not allow a new/unreviewed card to auto-publish when connectivity returns.\n\n### Presenter Console\nDesktop information hierarchy:\n\n1. **Persistent health strip**: audience display, network/server, device role/control owner, last sync/ack, timer.\n2. **Now (dominant)**: what the audience sees, speaker notes, timer, current evidence, primary transport.\n3. **Next**: next content/evidence preview and one-step reorder/hold.\n4. **Signals**: structured team cues, evidence requests, faults, and mentions.\n5. **Stable control dock**: Back, Next, Reveal/Hide evidence, Pause/Blackout, End. Separate End and require confirmation.\n\nEvery remote command exposes **Ready -> Sending -> Confirmed/Failed**. Do not optimistically imply the audience display changed before acknowledgment. Keyboard shortcuts supplement visible controls; they are never the only path. Preserve undo for reversible changes and provide a clear recovery action for failures.\n\n### Responsive second-device console\n- **>=1280 px**: Now / Next / Signals columns, with Now roughly half the workspace.\n- **~768–1279 px**: Now plus one contextual drawer; keep health and transport visible.\n- **320–767 px**: single-column Now; bottom tabs **Now / Next / Signals**; sticky thumb-reachable transport; faults appear above but do not move controls.\n- Avoid dense miniaturized cards. Collapse metadata behind a labeled disclosure, not tiny text.\n- Reflow without two-dimensional scrolling at 320 CSS px. Operational touch targets should be a stronger 44–48 px even though WCAG 2.2’s normative minimum is 24×24 px or sufficient spacing.\n- Maintain the same control labels/order across breakpoints to reduce mode errors under stress.\n\n### Team messaging\nTreat this as an operations channel, not generic chat-first collaboration.\n\n- Structured message types: **Urgent stage cue**, **Evidence request**, **Review decision**, **Ops**, with ordinary chat secondary.\n- Each signal has sender, timestamp, linked slide/evidence, severity label+icon, and **Acknowledge / Resolve / Pin**.\n- Live mode defaults to unresolved and mentioned items, batches low-priority arrivals, and offers a single Pause updates control.\n- Urgent signals persist until acknowledged; routine arrivals use a status region and never steal focus.\n- On phone, show a count and highest unresolved severity in the tab; do not cover transport controls or stage preview.\n- Provide quiet/rehearsal modes and an accessible history. Do not make users remember a cue from a disappearing toast.\n\n## 3. Setup wizard and Windows display readiness\nSix short, resumable steps:\n\n1. **Session**: title, audience, duration, privacy/recording expectation.\n2. **Content & evidence**: import, missing-media/source scan, approve initial evidence.\n3. **Displays**: detect screens; choose Audience vs Console; run visual/audio/caption test.\n4. **Devices & roles**: QR + short pairing code; label each device; assign Presenter/Producer/Researcher/Viewer; identify the single active control owner.\n5. **Offline readiness**: cache active deck/assets/evidence/notes; show exact version, size, timestamp; generate downloadable HTML/PDF emergency copy.\n6. **Rehearsal**: advance/back, evidence reveal, blackout, message/ack, disconnect/reconnect drill, then a clear readiness summary.\n\nReadiness uses **Pass / Warning / Blocked** with icon+text and a precise remedy. Allow an authorized bypass for warnings, record it, and never erase completed fields. Put help/recovery in a consistent location.\n\nWindows-specific copy should say:\n\n- Press **Win+P -> Extend** so public stage and private console differ; Duplicate is unsuitable for private notes.\n- Use **Win+Shift+Left/Right** to move the active browser window between monitors.\n- Use **Win+K** for Miracast/wireless display when needed.\n- Let the user click **Identify displays**, open the public window, move it, enter fullscreen, and confirm a numbered/color-safe test pattern from the room.\n- Verify projector scaling, resolution, browser zoom, audio output, captions, sleep/notification suppression, and fallback cable before “Ready.”\n\nMicrosoft references: https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/keyboard-shortcuts-in-windows-dcc61a57-8ff0-cffe-9796-cb9706c75eec and https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/screen-mirroring-and-projecting-to-your-pc-or-wireless-display-5af9f371-c704-1c7f-8f0d-fa607551d09c\n\n## 4. Cognitive load and accessibility constraints\n- Live console should answer only **What is on now? What happens next? Does anyone need me? Is the stage healthy?**\n- Keep primary controls fixed, label them with verbs, make control-to-effect relationships explicit, and prefer recognition over memory.\n- Limit interruptions, keep critical paths short, provide step-by-step instructions, avoid redundant entry, and preserve user input. W3C COGA: https://www.w3.org/TR/coga-usable/\n- Reflow at 320 CSS px without loss/2D scrolling: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/reflow.html\n- Pointer target baseline 24×24 px or sufficient spacing; product target 44–48 px for live controls: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/target-size-minimum.html\n- Visible focus indicator equivalent to a 2 CSS px perimeter at >=3:1 contrast: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/focus-appearance.html\n- Body text contrast >=4.5:1; large text >=3:1: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html\n- Never use color as the only status/severity signal: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/use-of-color.html\n- Auto-updating feeds need pause/stop/hide or frequency control: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/pause-stop-hide.html\n- Routine connection/sync changes are programmatic status messages without focus movement: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/status-messages.html\n- Use persistent `role=alert` only for brief, important stage-stopping faults; alerts do not steal focus or auto-disappear: https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/alert/\n- Keep help in a consistent relative location: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/consistent-help.html\n- Do not request the same setup information twice: https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/redundant-entry.html\n\n## 5. Degraded/offline state model\nModel cause and capability separately. Every degraded state says: **what happened; what still works; what is paused; last successful sync/ack; recommended action**.\n\n| State | Public Stage | Console/team | Recovery |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Slow network | Continue current frame; defer nonessential media | Delay thumbnails/analytics; show latency | Retry in background, preserve controls |\n| Device offline | Cached deck/content continues | Cached notes/timer work; messages/evidence stale/read-only; local drafts pending | Manual retry plus online event; show last sync |\n| Server unreachable | Continue local cached presentation | Remote publish/messaging disabled or explicitly pending | Health check; never imply server ack |\n| Realtime channel lost | Freeze confirmed session state | Show disconnected; reject/hold remote-only commands | Fetch canonical snapshot on reconnect |\n| Audience display disconnected | Freeze last valid audience frame if still rendered | Persistent stage-critical fault | Identify/reopen display; local fallback window |\n| Cache/assets missing | Do not claim offline-ready | Block readiness with exact missing items | Re-cache or download emergency HTML/PDF |\n| App update ready | Current version remains active | “Update after session” | Activate only after session ends |\n\nCaching strategy: versioned cache-first app shell and active presentation assets; network-first with last-known cache for recent messages/evidence; local durable drafts for notes. Sources: https://web.dev/learn/pwa/serving/ , https://web.dev/articles/offline-fallback-page , https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Progressive_web_apps/Guides/Caching , https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Service_Worker_API/Using_Service_Workers\n\nOn reconnect: fetch canonical snapshot; deduplicate by idempotency key; show a compact reconciliation summary; sync safe notes/messages; discard stale slide/publication commands by default; require confirmation before sending any queued action that changes public output. Never replay a burst of old “next/reveal” commands.\n\n## 6. Post-session report\nDefault report sections:\n\n1. **Outcome summary**: duration, completion, presenters/team, reliability summary.\n2. **What the audience saw**: ordered timeline of content/evidence with exact timestamps.\n3. **Evidence ledger**: source, reviewer, status, where shown, conflicts/limitations, accessible links.\n4. **Decisions & cues**: acknowledgments/resolutions; private team chat excluded by default.\n5. **Incidents**: disconnects, recovery action, duration, lost/queued data, app/deck version.\n6. **Audience signals**: only consented/minimized data, with tables and text summaries—not chart-only output.\n7. **Actions**: unresolved evidence, follow-ups, owner/due date.\n8. **Export/share**: private full report vs sanitized public report; HTML/CSV and tagged accessible PDF where feasible.\n\nLabel machine-generated summaries **Draft—review before sharing**. Separate measured facts from inferred insights. Preserve timezone and clock source, make every chart available as a table, and make public sharing an explicit sanitization step rather than a toggle that might leak messages/notes.\n\n## 7. Product acceptance tests worth carrying into implementation\n- Presenter can pair a phone, lose network, advance a cached deck, regain network, and understand exactly what did/did not sync.\n- Audience display never exposes private notes/messages during setup, failure, refresh, or fullscreen exit.\n- A keyboard-only presenter can complete setup, operate live controls, triage a fault, and end the session with visible focus.\n- At 320 CSS px and 200% zoom, all console functionality remains available without 2D scrolling.\n- A color-blind user can distinguish every evidence/health/message state without hue.\n- Screen-reader output announces routine status without focus movement and announces one persistent critical fault without repeated alert spam.\n- Win+P Extend rehearsal verifies the correct public/console monitor and survives reconnect/reopen.\n- Reconnection does not replay stale public commands or auto-publish unreviewed evidence.\n- Post-session public export contains no private messages, notes, device identifiers, or unapproved evidence.\n\n## Expansion leads\n- Field-test with a presenter, producer, AV technician, keyboard/screen-reader user, low-vision user, and a situationally impaired presenter under time pressure.\n- Measure projector readability at realistic room distance, ambient light, low-quality HDMI/Miracast, browser zoom, and 16:9/4:3 mismatch.\n- Specify the multi-device authority protocol: control ownership, command acknowledgment, idempotency, clock skew, conflict resolution, and handoff.\n- Validate browser/OS support for opening/placing fullscreen audience windows; design explicit fallbacks where window-management APIs are unavailable.\n- Define evidence trust policy: source allow/deny rules, review permissions, conflict display, stale-source warnings, and public attribution rules.\n- Define privacy/retention: session codes, audience telemetry, message retention, report sanitization, deletion, and export audit trail.\n- Run an offline matrix across Edge/Chrome on Windows, installed vs browser PWA, cache eviction, server outage, websocket loss, sleep/wake, and service-worker update.\n- Prototype and test captions, live-region verbosity, reduced motion, high contrast/forced-colors, 200–400% zoom, touch, switch, and voice input.\n- Validate accessible report export, especially tagged PDF, table structure, links, reading order, and alternate text.\n\n## EXPAND"}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":1422,"output":4009,"cacheRead":93696,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":745,"totalTokens":99127,"cost":{"input":0.007110000000000001,"output":0.12027,"cacheRead":0.046848,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.174228}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1786648908762,"responseId":"resp_002a496e23ec3bf4016a7e194d2298819191afb82adea981c6","rawStopReason":"completed"}}
